<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Emperor Shengzong of Liao</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/971">971</a>–<a href="/years/1031">1031</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Yelü Longxu · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liao-dynasty">Liao dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Emperor Shengzong of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liao-dynasty">Liao</a>, Yelü Longxu, was the sixth emperor of the Khitan Liao dynasty, reigning from 982 to 1031. During his long reign, at first under the regency of his mother, Empress Dowager <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiao-yanyan">Xiao Chuo</a>, Liao reached its height: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chanyuan-treaty">Chanyuan Treaty</a> of 1005 secured annual payments from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a>, and campaigns were fought against Goryeo and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jurchens">Jurchen</a>. He also extended Chinese-style administration and examinations.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Shengzong's personal name was Wenshunu. He succeeded his father, Emperor Jingzong, in 982 while still a boy, and because he was too young to rule his mother, Empress Dowager Xiao, became regent. Emperor Taizong of the Northern Song dynasty tried to exploit the situation in 986 by sending three large armies against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liao-dynasty">Liao</a> southern capital, present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>, in the contested <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sixteen-prefectures">Sixteen Prefectures</a>. The young emperor and his mother led Liao cavalry against them and defeated the Song at the Battle of the Qigou Pass in June.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>The empress dowager made Yelü Xiuge her senior general, and attacks on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> went on into the following year. In 1004 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liao-dynasty">Liao</a> invaded Song territory on a large scale and camped at Shanyuan, about 100 miles north of the Song capital, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaifeng">Kaifeng</a>. Under the treaty signed in mid-January 1005 the Song paid 200,000 bolts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> and 100,000 taels of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> each year in exchange for peace. With modifications the arrangement lasted until the end of the Liao, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jurchen-jin-dynasty">Jurchen Jin dynasty</a> later continued it with the Song.</p><p>Examinations for officials on the Tang and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> model were institutionalised in 988. At first only three to five candidates passed; by 1014 between 30 and 130 passed the triennial examinations. Most holders of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jinshi">jinshi</a> degree were never appointed, since Khitan aristocrats, who were barred from the examinations and rose through patronage, were far more likely to receive office. A feudal reform freed most slaves, and the economy came to rest on raising horses and sheep, on agriculture and on fishing rather than on expansion and plunder.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Shengzong began the active patronage of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> and rebuilt temples such as the Monastery of Solitary Joy. By one estimate, perhaps exaggerated, a tenth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liao-dynasty">Liao</a> population were monks or nuns within a century of his reign. Most shrines stood in the south among the settled Chinese population, and the Khitan seem to have kept their animistic beliefs and rituals. He died on 25 June 1031 as the longest-reigning monarch of the Liao, and his reign is widely praised as the dynasty's golden age of peace and prosperity.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Emperor Shengzong of Liao” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Buddhism flourished under imperial patronage, especially under <b>Shengzong</b> and Daozong; monasteries held great estates, and the Liao printed their own edition of the Buddhist canon.</blockquote><a href="/empire/liao-dynasty">Liao dynasty · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Emperor Shengzong of Liao</b> sent a campaign in 985 that destroyed the kingdom by 986.</blockquote><a href="/state/dingan">Dingan</a></li><li><blockquote>It was formally abolished around 982 under Emperor <b>Shengzong</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/dongdan">Dongdan</a></li></ul></section>
